{% callout type="summary" %}
**Quick answer:** Skeletal muscle contracts when a motor nerve releases acetylcholine, which triggers a muscle action potential. The action potential makes the sarcoplasmic reticulum release calcium. Calcium binds troponin C, uncovers the binding sites on actin, and myosin heads pull the actin filaments inward using ATP. This is the sliding filament mechanism. Pumping calcium back relaxes the muscle.
{% /callout %}

{% cbmeTags codes="PY3.7" phase="Phase 1" /%}

## Sequence of events in skeletal muscle

The initiation and execution of contraction follow these steps:

1. An **action potential** travels along a motor nerve to its endings on muscle fibres.
2. At each ending the nerve secretes a small amount of **acetylcholine (ACh)**.
3. ACh acts on a local area of the muscle fibre membrane and opens **ACh-gated cation channels**.
4. Large amounts of **sodium** diffuse into the fibre. This local depolarization opens voltage-gated sodium channels and starts an action potential.
5. The action potential travels along the muscle fibre membrane in the same way as along a nerve fibre.
6. The action potential depolarizes the membrane, and its electrical current flows through the centre of the fibre. This makes the **sarcoplasmic reticulum** release the large amounts of **calcium** it stores.
7. Calcium starts the attractive forces between actin and myosin filaments, so they **slide** past each other. This is the contractile process.
8. After a fraction of a second, a **calcium pump** returns the calcium to the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Removing calcium from the myofibrils ends the contraction.

## Neuromuscular junction

The motor end-plate is where a motor nerve meets a muscle fibre.

- An action potential reaching the end-plate causes ACh to be released from storage vesicles into a highly infolded **synaptic cleft** 30 to 50 nm wide.
- ACh binds quickly to receptors in the **junctional folds**. This almost instantly raises the permeability of the postsynaptic membrane and produces a local depolarization, the **end-plate potential**.
- The end-plate potential starts an action potential in the surrounding sarcolemma.
- The action of ACh is quickly ended by the enzyme **acetylcholinesterase (AChE)**, bound to the basal lamina in the junctional folds.

The sarcolemma is excitable, so action potentials spread rapidly over the whole surface of the fibre.

At rest, small amounts of ACh are released all the time. These produce **miniature end-plate potentials**, which are small, brief potentials seen most clearly when a recording needle lies near an end-plate.

## Excitation-contraction coupling

Action potentials are carried into the interior of the fibre by the **T tubules**, which are extensions of the sarcolemma. This activates all parts of the fibre rapidly and almost together.

**Excitation-contraction coupling** is the process by which an action potential triggers calcium release from the **terminal cisternae of the sarcoplasmic reticulum** into the cytosol. Calcium then activates a calcium-sensitive switch in the thin filaments and starts contraction. Fast-contracting fibres have especially extensive sarcoplasmic reticulum.

Calcium not only starts contraction. It also activates the efflux systems that bring contraction to a close.

## Structure of the contractile machinery

From large to small, the arrangement is: muscle fascicle, muscle fibre, **myofibril**, **sarcomere**. A sarcomere lies between two Z lines (Z disks), with an M line in the middle.

- **Thin filament:** actin, made of two helical strands of F-actin, with **tropomyosin** lying in the grooves between the strands and the **troponin complex** attached.
- **Thick filament:** myosin. Each myosin molecule has a tail and a hinged head (cross-bridge head, called S-1). The thick filament has opposite polarities at its two ends, separated by a 150 nm central segment, the M band, which does not form cross-bridges.
- **Titin:** each molecule extends from the Z disk to the M line. One end is elastic and attached to the Z disk, acting as a spring that changes length as the sarcomere contracts and relaxes. The other part tethers to the thick filament. It may also act as a template for forming parts of the contractile filaments.

## Sliding filament mechanism

In the relaxed sarcomere, the ends of the actin filaments from two successive Z disks barely overlap. In the contracted state, the actin filaments are pulled inward among the myosin filaments so that they overlap to the maximum, and the Z disks are pulled to the ends of the myosin filaments. Muscle contraction therefore occurs by **sliding of filaments**, not by the filaments themselves shortening.

{% figure src="/learn-images/physiology/muscle-contraction/sarcomere-sliding-filament.webp" alt="Sarcomere in relaxed and contracted states showing actin and myosin sliding" caption="The sarcomere shortens as thin filaments slide between thick filaments." credit="David Richfield, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY-SA 3.0" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sarcomere.svg" width="960" height="743" /%}

## How calcium switches contraction on and off

Skeletal muscle is regulated by the **troponin system**, bound to tropomyosin and F-actin in the thin filament. This is called **actin-based regulation**. The troponin complex has three parts: troponin T, troponin I and troponin C.

{% figure src="/learn-images/physiology/muscle-contraction/troponin-tropomyosin-calcium.webp" alt="Thin filament with troponin and tropomyosin blocking or exposing myosin binding sites" caption="Calcium binding troponin moves tropomyosin and exposes the binding sites on actin." credit="Daniel Walsh and Alan Sved, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY-SA 4.0" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Actin-tropomyosin-troponin.png" width="960" height="762" /%}

- **At rest:** troponin I stops the myosin head from binding actin, either by changing the shape of F-actin through tropomyosin or by rolling tropomyosin into a position that blocks the binding sites.
- **Calcium arrives:** each troponin C can bind strongly with up to four calcium ions. The troponin complex changes shape and moves tropomyosin deeper into the groove between the actin strands. This **uncovers the active sites** on actin so myosin heads can attach.
- **Calcium falls:** the troponin C and calcium complex dissociates, troponin I restores its block, the filaments detach and the muscle relaxes.

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Switch order:** calcium binds troponin C, which relieves the block by troponin I, and the power stroke can begin. Without calcium, troponin I blocks actin.
{% /callout %}

## Cross-bridge cycle (walk-along theory)

Once actin is activated, the myosin heads are attracted to its active sites. The best-supported hypothesis is the **walk-along (ratchet) theory**. The cycle is:

1. **Attachment:** the myosin head, charged with ADP and phosphate (Pi), binds actin.
2. **Power stroke:** Pi is released, then ADP is released. This causes a large change in the shape of the myosin head, pulling the actin about 5 to 10 nm toward the centre of the sarcomere. The head is now in a low-energy, nucleotide-free state.
3. **Detachment:** a new ATP binds the head, which sharply lowers its affinity for actin, so actin is released.
4. **Re-cocking:** the head hydrolyzes ATP to ADP and Pi, which stay bound. This raises myosin into a high-energy position, ready for another cycle if calcium stays high.

Each step is tiny in distance and energy, but a human biceps contains an enormous number of myosin molecules, so the sum gives both great force and rapid movement. The thermodynamic efficiency of contraction is about 50%, compared with 20% or less for an internal combustion engine.

### Rigor mortis

After death, intracellular ATP falls. Without ATP, the myosin head cannot detach from actin, so the body stiffens.

## Energy and the roles of ATP

ATP is used to drive movement of the filaments. It binds the myosin heads and is hydrolyzed to ADP and Pi by the ATPase activity of the actomyosin complex. ATP has two jobs: it is needed to **detach** the myosin head from actin, and its hydrolysis re-cocks the head.

## Smooth and cardiac muscle: how regulation differs

Calcium is central in all muscle, but the regulation differs.

{% table %}
- **Feature**
- **Skeletal (striated) muscle**
- **Smooth muscle**
---
- **Type of regulation**
- Actin-based, through the troponin system
- Myosin-based, through the regulatory light chains of myosin
---
- **Block released by**
- Calcium binding troponin C
- Phosphorylation of the regulatory light chains by myosin light-chain kinase (activated by calcium and calmodulin)
---
- **Source of calcium**
- The sarcoplasmic reticulum
- Mainly inflow through calcium channels in the sarcolemma (this is also major in cardiac muscle)
{% /table %}

## Length-tension relationship

The tension a muscle produces depends on how much actin and myosin overlap, because tension is proportional to the number of cross-links between them.

- At the longest sarcomere length there is no overlap, and active tension is **zero**.
- As the sarcomere shortens and overlap increases, tension rises until the sarcomere is about **2.2 micrometres** long, when actin overlaps all the cross-bridges.
- Full tension is kept down to about **2 micrometres**.
- From 2 down to about 1.65 micrometres, the actin ends start to overlap each other and tension falls.
- Stretching beyond about 2.2 micrometres also reduces active tension.

In experiments, **active tension** is the total tension minus the passive tension at that length. Active tension rises with length, reaches a peak and then falls. **Passive tension** always increases as the muscle is stretched. The **resting length** is the length at which active tension is maximal.

## Force-velocity relationship

A skeletal muscle with no load contracts to full contraction in about 0.1 second. As the load rises, the speed of shortening falls. When the load equals the maximum force the muscle can produce, the speed becomes zero and no shortening occurs even though the muscle is active. The reason is that the load is a reverse force that reduces the net force available to shorten the muscle.

## Isometric and isotonic contraction

{% table %}
- **Type**
- **Muscle length**
- **Examples**
---
- **Isometric**
- Stays the same; tension rises without shortening
- Postural (antigravity) muscles during standing; trying to lift a weight that does not move
---
- **Isotonic**
- Shortens clearly during contraction
- Lifting a weight
{% /table %}

## Motor units and summation

A **motor unit** is a single motor neuron and all the muscle fibres it supplies.

- **Small motor units** give a high degree of independent control but low strength. Examples are the finger and extraocular muscles.
- **Large motor units** give less fine control but high strength and coordination. Examples are biceps and quadriceps.
- As more force is needed, motor units are recruited, starting with the small ones.
- After nerve damage, remaining axons sprout new branches that reinnervate fibres, so motor units grow larger.

**Summation** increases the force of contraction in two ways:

1. **Multiple fibre summation:** more motor units contract at the same time.
2. **Frequency summation:** a higher frequency of stimulation, which can lead to **tetanization**.

With a stimulus of increasing strength: a **subthreshold** stimulus gives no response, a **threshold** stimulus is the minimum that just evokes a response, a **maximal** stimulus gives the maximum response, and a **supramaximal** stimulus is stronger but does not increase the contraction further. Contraction grows with stimulus strength because more motor units are recruited.

## Fatigue

Neuromuscular transmission can fall a little after intense, prolonged activity, which reduces contraction further. If blood flow to a contracting muscle is cut off, fatigue is almost complete within 1 or 2 minutes, because of the loss of nutrients, especially oxygen.

## Applied points

- **Malignant hyperthermia:** many cases are due to mutations in the calcium release channel (ryanodine receptor). Muscle behaves normally until certain anaesthetic agents, or rarely high heat or strenuous exercise, trigger abnormal calcium release, causing sustained contraction and heat production. Severe cases can be fatal.
- **Contracture:** fixed muscle shortening. In physiological contracture, the muscle stays shortened for many minutes because the metabolic mechanism for relaxation has failed. It is seen in McArdle disease (phosphorylase deficiency) and phosphofructokinase deficiency. The EMG stays relatively silent, unlike in cramp or tetany.
- **Myotonia:** a disorder of the muscle membrane. Tone is normal at rest, but after contraction the muscle relaxes slowly. In grip myotonia the patient cannot let go of an object. Percussion myotonia is elicited by tapping the muscle.
- **Fasciculation:** a local twitch of a small bundle of fibres. It is usually benign but can be due to motor neuron disease. **Clonus** is a series of rhythmic contractions triggered by the stretch reflex, with larger movements, and often indicates an upper motor neuron lesion.
- **Muscle wasting:** after disuse the fibres are intact but thinner. After denervation they degenerate and are replaced by fibrous tissue or fat.
- **Power grading:** grade 0 is no contraction, 1 a flicker, 2 movement with gravity eliminated, 3 movement against gravity, 4 movement against gravity and resistance, and 5 normal power.

## Practice MCQs

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="Calcium released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum binds troponin C, which relieves the block by troponin I and uncovers the active sites on actin." %}
Which molecule does calcium bind to start contraction in skeletal muscle?
- A. Tropomyosin
- B. Troponin C
- C. Troponin I
- D. Titin
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="D" explanation="A new ATP binds the myosin head and sharply lowers its affinity for actin, so the head detaches. Without ATP the heads stay attached, as in rigor mortis." %}
Detachment of the myosin head from actin requires:
- A. Calcium binding to myosin
- B. Release of ADP
- C. Release of phosphate
- D. Binding of a new ATP molecule
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="A" explanation="The calcium release channel in the sarcoplasmic reticulum is the ryanodine receptor. Mutations cause abnormal release of calcium and sustained contraction with heat when triggered by certain anaesthetic agents." %}
Malignant hyperthermia is due mainly to a defect in:
- A. The calcium release channel of the sarcoplasmic reticulum
- B. Acetylcholinesterase
- C. The acetylcholine receptor
- D. Troponin T
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="Tension depends on the number of actin-myosin cross-links. Tension is zero when there is no overlap, and it is maximal at about 2.2 to 2 micrometres where actin overlaps all the cross-bridges." %}
Active tension is greatest when:
- A. There is no overlap of actin and myosin
- B. The sarcomere is stretched far beyond resting length
- C. Actin overlaps all the myosin cross-bridges
- D. The actin filaments overlap each other fully
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="AChE bound to the basal lamina in the junctional folds rapidly breaks down ACh, ending its action." %}
The action of acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction is terminated by:
- A. Reuptake into the nerve ending
- B. Acetylcholinesterase
- C. Diffusion of ACh into the T tubules
- D. Troponin
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="D" explanation="In smooth muscle, regulation is myosin-based. Calcium with calmodulin activates myosin light-chain kinase, which phosphorylates the regulatory light chains and releases the block." %}
In smooth muscle, contraction is regulated mainly by:
- A. Troponin C
- B. Troponin I
- C. Tropomyosin movement
- D. Phosphorylation of myosin regulatory light chains
{% /mcq %}

## Viva questions

{% viva question="What is the sliding filament mechanism?" %}
During contraction the actin filaments are pulled inward among the myosin filaments, so the overlap increases and the Z disks move toward the ends of the myosin filaments. The filaments slide; they do not shorten.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Describe the steps from nerve impulse to contraction." %}
Motor nerve action potential, ACh release, opening of ACh-gated channels, sodium entry, muscle action potential, spread along T tubules, calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, calcium binding troponin C, cross-bridge cycling.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What does ATP do in muscle contraction?" %}
It binds the myosin head to cause detachment from actin, and its hydrolysis to ADP and Pi re-cocks the head into a high-energy position for the next cycle.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What causes relaxation?" %}
Calcium is pumped back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum. The troponin C and calcium complex dissociates, troponin I blocks the actin sites again, and the filaments detach.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Why does rigor mortis occur?" %}
ATP falls after death, so the myosin head cannot detach from actin, and the muscle stays stiff.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Define a motor unit and give examples of small and large units." %}
A motor neuron and all the muscle fibres it supplies. Small units (finger, extraocular muscles) give fine control; large units (biceps, quadriceps) give strength.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Compare isometric and isotonic contraction." %}
In isometric contraction the muscle length stays the same while tension rises, as in standing posture. In isotonic contraction the muscle shortens, as in lifting a weight.
{% /viva %}

